    "lawCode": "Amd §459-c, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2440\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to exempting\nfrom real property taxation real property owned by a person certified\nto receive a United States department of veterans affairs disability\npension\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nExempts from real property taxation real property owned by a person\ncertified to receive a United States department of veterans affairs\ndisability pension.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nThis bill amends Section 459-c (2) (b) to add the receipt of a United\nStates Department of Veterans Affairs disability pension and the\nletter one receives, is evidence that the person is disabled and has\nlimited income.\n\n EFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER :\nExisting law recognizes several forms of proof of disability, but does\nnot specifically recognize a veteran's disability pension. This bill\nsimply adds further clarification to the existing law without altering\nthe actual definition of a person with a disability and limited\nincome.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThis section of law was recently amended by Chapter 421 of the Laws of\n2000 to include the receipt of a U.S. Postal Service Disability\nPension as qualifying proof of disability. This bill adds the\nveteran's disability pension pursuant to 38 U.S.C. section 1521 to the\nlist of approved persons who are eligible to receive this exemption.\n\nThe VA Disability Pension is a benefit to wartime veterans with\nlimited income who are no longer able to work. One may be eligible if\nthey were discharged from service other than dishonorable conditions,\nserved 90 days or more of active duty with at least 1 day during a\nperiod of war, have disabilities that keep you from working a regular,\nfull-time job and the countable family income is below a yearly limit\nset by law.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 Passed Assembly.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
